it's great to welcome Jessica piccolo  back to the program she is a senior  legal analyst for rewire which is a  daily online publication covering  reproductive justice today we are going  to talk about masterpiece cake shop the  Colorado Civil Rights Commission which  might be known to you as the gay wedding  cake case which is now before the  Supreme Court  so Jessica as with many of these types  of cases we can't even seem to get  agreement from from two sides at least  about what the case is about there are  some who say this is about anti LGBT  discrimination and others who say that  it is a case about religious freedom so  for people who don't know even the facts  of the case what is this about sure so  I'll give you guys a quick rundown on of  the facts so there's a couple who  same-sex couple who walked into  masterpiece cake shop they were looking  for a cake to celebrate their wedding  and they had been married in  Massachusetts and we're a Colorado  couple and so wanted to celebrate in  Colorado and Phillips who's the owner of  the cake shop refused to sell them a  cake once he realized basically who they  were and what they wanted and he said  you know I I won't sell a cake to you I  won't make a cake to you because I have  a religious objection to same-sex  marriage the couple filed a complaint  with the Colorado Commission of Human  Rights the Commission did an  investigation and found that the refusal  to serve the couple was in violation  with Colorado's anti-discrimination  statute in Colorado like a lot of other  state has an anti-discrimination statute  that says affectively you cannot  discriminate against folks on all of the  usual characteristics that we have in  civil rights laws so that would be you  know race national origin age but the  Colorado civil rights law as do many of  the states goes further and specifically  includes sexual orientation and so the  Commission very rightly said this is a  problem  Phillips appealed and the court of  appeals said it you know reaffirmed the  Commission's decision and then Alliance  defending freedom who represents  Phillips and and their conservative  litigation  grupe they filed a brief with the  Supreme Court the Supreme Court sat on  the case 19 times before taking it up so  that in and of itself is very  interesting so that's Philips side the  Commission side and has been argued by  the folks who have filed amici on behalf  of the couple detail the very long  history that this country has of  religious evangelicals in particular  trying to launch religious objections to  civil rights laws the Supreme Court has  already looked at this as an issue in  the case of Bob Jones V the United  States where Bob Jones University tried  to defend its decision to not allow  housing for interracial couples on the  grounds that it had a biblical objection  to that and the United States Supreme  Court said it was fine for the  government to pull its non profit tax  funding status in that case so this  should be a no-brainer the fact that it  is not is something that advocates  should pay attention to so there is an  argument that some defenders of the cake  baker are making which is that this is  not equivalent to we do not serve gay  people in our business they are saying  they have the option to participate or  not in the baking of cakes for gay  weddings which are a type of event and  that they are not actually refusing  service to the couple on the basis of  their identity that they're refusing  service on the basis that it's a type of  event for which they don't work is that  legally a valid distinction or argument  well I think it's a pretty facetious  argument on I mean I will you know i  anecdotal anecdotes aren't evidence but  I will just say for example that when I  got married I hired a bakery to make our  wedding cake and in no way shape or form  did they participate in the ceremony or  in any way do anything that suggested  they were endorsing the union that I was  entering into the folks who are  defending the Baker and Phillips has  made this argument as well are trying to  say that he's an artist and what he's  doing is purely expressive speech and  and you know as Justice Sonia Sotomayor  pointed out in oral arguments well there  are people  who consider themselves sandwich artists  does that mean if you have subway cater  your student event that they are somehow  endorsing the message of it it's really  I mean when you start to drill down into  it  Ludacris what about the idea and I'm  just thinking out loud here I mean I I  happen to think that this this should  that the Baker is wrong here right but  but in the or in in the interest of  playing devil's advocate and really  thinking through I was imagining a  scenario where a heterosexual couple  right maybe it's someone related to one  of the two men who were going to get  married if they were to come in and say  that they were buying a cake for the gay  wedding and they were of course a  heterosexual couple and the individual  Baker said I don't do cakes for gay  weddings for example would that be  different because again it's not refute  the couple that they're refusing to  provide something for that's coming into  place the order would be a heterosexual  couple but the eventual use of the cake  would be in a gay wedding does that  example support the Baker's argument in  some way it's a really excellent  hypothetical and I'm glad you asked it  because what it allows us to do is  really talk about you know the who or  versus the what is how I like to  describe this case right is an objection  to who or to what and your your example  flips that because you're absolutely  right it's not the Baker launching an  objection against the person but  refusing to participate in a ceremony if  they were fine part like giving the the  heterosexual couple or the the  heterosexual person the cake on their  behalf  it immediately undercuts their argument  because it is absolutely then about who  they are comfortable about serving they  claim it's about the what but you know  I'm not sure if that does that you know  that distinction it slicing the Apple  kind of thin but in my mind that  actually helps the couples case because  what it you know in that sense because  if if they agree to serve a heterosexual  person or a you know a heterosexual  couple regardless of what the event is  and even if it's event that they say  they object to then they're not  participating in that event with any  you know real you know it undercuts  their participation argument I was  actually thinking of the opposite which  is if they said even to the heterosexual  couple it's for gay weddings and that  that would actually in some sense  strengthen their argument in either case  we're talking about hypotheticals though  sure sure sure I mean I certainly think  that that is a compelling a more  compelling point to the Baker side  because it is again this idea of  objecting to or putting forward that  objection but it doesn't change the end  result that what they are objecting to  is something that is a legal right and  that is a legal right held by a  traditionally discriminated against  class of people so you can dress it up  in different ways but in terms of the  outcome it is still saying I have a  religious objection to a constitutional  right that you have and historically the  law has just not recognized that and  with good reason  yeah so that's kind of the bigger  picture here which is with a lot of  these types of legal debates you have a  sort of budding heads of freedom from  and freedom to in the sense that the  Colorado non-discrimination law by  including sexual orientation as a  protected class says that individuals  who are part of that members of that  class should be free from discrimination  and those on the other side say that our  religious freedom gives us freedom to  sort of exist in society in accordance  with our religious laws isn't that  really what's at issue here it is except  I would take that further because the  the balance that you described is  absolutely an important one and one that  traditionally has not been an issue  aside from what the the evangelicals  have pushed because it is possible to  recognize a myriad of religious beliefs  and also recognize the fundamental civil  rights of people in this country so I  think there's there's that part of it  and then in terms of really the larger  piece I would say that we this is an  example and it's been increasing  really since 2010 it's sort of gone on  hyper  under the Trump administration of  Evangelicals trying to use religious  objections and quite frankly weaponized  the First Amendment as both a sword to  puncture through civil rights laws and a  shield from the liability that happens  when they do so I mean frankly we saw  this with this setup with Hobby Lobby  they have great opening to do so now  yeah that was I mean when the Supreme  Court decided that state bans on  same-sex marriage were not going to fly  it was abundantly clear at that point  and the Hobby Lobby case was one of the  big notable examples that the shift in  the battle against LGBT rights was going  to move to religious freedom and and  still by the way I mentioned this  anytime that there's an opportunity to I  don't remember the number of states but  in many states you can still be fired  for your perceived or actual membership  identification as an LGBT individual and  that is a completely separate issue from  that of same-sex marriage and I don't  know if you have thoughts about where  where that's going oh absolutely the  Supreme Court just this week refused to  hear a case on that exact issue there's  currently what we call in the law a  circuit split so federal courts of  appeals are not in agreement as to  whether or not firing someone from the  workplace because they are gay amounts  to discrimination on the basis of sex  under title seven of the Civil Rights  Act which is the federal employment law  um so that is very much an open question  for debate now you know I mean it's  always reading the tea leaves in terms  of why the Supreme Court decides to take  up a case and why they don't you know I  I wrote a quick piece on this in terms  of why this particular case that they  considered may not have been a really  good candidate but this is absolutely an  issue we are going to see because  exactly what you mentioned in many  states is still possible to lose your  job because you're gay and there is you  know or it goes even further in terms of  like certain gender non-conforming  behaviors and stereotypes so you know  that in addition to the fight over how  the law recognizes the rights of  transgender people to is is really the  burgeoning civil rights battle that we  are seeing right now last thing to touch  on as far as this case goes that we we  started today's discussion about when  might we expect to  here a decision and what ultimately will  the decision hinge on in your mind okay  so the court was positively packed with  press to the point where we were given a  signed seat so this is likely the  biggest case or one of it's certainly  one of the biggest cases on the courts  term so that usually means an end of  June decision particularly since they  conferenced on it so long I think this  is going to be one to hash out I would  like to see it earlier in the sense that  I would hope be hopeful that it's an  easy question for the court to answer in  terms of what it hinges on as we have  seen time and time again a lot of the  outcome of this case hinges on Justice  Anthony Kennedy he has two real  competing legacies here he has the key  has his legacy of advancing LGBTQ rights  even if it has been slowly but you know  that's how the law works and really  working to insulate religious  individuals and in particular religious  conservatives to you know I mean I mean  he's he's the Hobby Lobby decision to  allow them space to exist in civil  rights laws that they did not have a  space to exist before and with good  reason and so we'll see where he comes  out in this case he was very concerned  about using civil rights laws to  discriminate against the LGBTQ  population  he was as concerned that ruling against  the Baker would somehow endorse what was  argued by the Baker as religious  discrimination by the State against  evangelicals so it's gonna be it's gonna  be a closed case I would expect a pretty  split along ideological lines decision  all right we will be following it we've  been speaking with Jessica picolo senior  legal analyst for rewire great to talk  to you again thank you so much for  having me back always such a pleasure

